WEBVTT

00:06.901 --> 00:12.304
I've done it before where I only pushed it once and I thought it was recording and I did a five-hour interview.

00:13.024 --> 00:14.245
And none of it was recording.

00:14.265 --> 00:15.326
None of it was recording.

00:15.686 --> 00:16.106
Oh, geez.

00:16.266 --> 00:16.526
Yeah.

00:16.746 --> 00:17.107
Yes.

00:17.307 --> 00:19.088
So today is May 8th, 2026.

00:19.108 --> 00:22.409
My name is Caitlyn Sheets and I am here with Dr. G. Cliff Lamb.

00:22.669 --> 00:29.013
We're conducting this interview in the AgLife Science Building on the campus of Texas A&M University in College Station, Texas.

00:29.453 --> 00:32.895
The interview is part of the Coral History Project documenting the experiences of Aggies.

00:33.281 --> 00:33.381
Yes.

00:33.401 --> 00:33.501
Yes.

00:49.338 --> 01:16.301
sure yeah so yeah thanks a lot for taking the time um to chat so i have a very unique background i actually grew up in zimbabwe africa so south central africa on a beef cattle dairy corn and tobacco operation and so throughout my whole career i was very interested in agriculture i knew at a very young age i was going to come to the u.s and study

01:17.302 --> 01:29.013
And so in the late 80s, I came and I studied at Middle Tennessee State University, got my bachelor's degree, and then went to Kansas State and got my master's and PhD.

01:30.455 --> 01:33.457
I've always been highly passionate in reproductive biology.

01:34.318 --> 01:52.093
and using reproduction to increase efficiency of livestock operations so so that's uh that was my uh formal education and then from there i had full intentions to go back to africa at that point that was always my goal and in the late 1990s early 2000s

01:54.355 --> 02:01.862
there was a big push by the government of Zimbabwe to nationalize all of the farms and ranches, or most of them.

02:02.583 --> 02:08.088
So basically, the operation I grew up on and many of the operations

02:09.888 --> 02:11.549
those operations were nationalized.

02:11.610 --> 02:22.759
And so there was not a real large incentive for me to return to Africa because we didn't really have anything that we could sort of take over and operate.

02:22.799 --> 02:28.484
And so I took a position at the University of Minnesota as a faculty member.

02:29.389 --> 02:33.352
I spent 10 years as a faculty member at the University of Minnesota and loved it.

02:34.053 --> 02:35.194
Really enjoyed being there.

02:36.195 --> 02:44.422
Following that, I was asked to essentially do some work as an administrator and a faculty member at the University of Florida.

02:45.342 --> 02:47.343
And I was there for about 10 years.

02:48.143 --> 02:53.144
And then about, I'm in my 10th year now at Texas A&M.

02:54.104 --> 03:05.187
And, you know, I came here as the department head in March of 2017 as department head of animal science.

03:06.508 --> 03:19.401
I spent five years as department head of animal science and also interim department head for ecology and conservation biology, another department on campus.

03:19.962 --> 03:24.046
At that time, that department was restructured with wildlife and fisheries.

03:24.757 --> 03:49.035
into two separate departments and i served as the interim department head for rangeland wildlife and fisheries management at the same time as being department head of animal science yeah it sure is and uh but it but a great experience and then uh in december of 2021 the chancellor of the texas amy university system had called me and asked me if i would

03:49.715 --> 03:53.156
be interested in being the director of AgriLife Research.

03:53.196 --> 04:02.457
And so I saw a lot of opportunity, a lot of opportunity to grow our agency and took that position.

04:02.497 --> 04:06.218
And I've been in this role since January of 2022.

04:06.258 --> 04:07.778
Fabulous.

04:08.298 --> 04:11.619
Many roles and much experience.

04:11.799 --> 04:17.820
For someone who is ignorant, going back a little bit on what you're talking about, what does it mean when

04:18.475 --> 04:21.396
they took the farms, the ranches, and made it state?

04:22.517 --> 04:27.139
Yeah, so they nationalized about 6,000 farms and ranches.

04:27.399 --> 04:30.760
In other words, the government took them away from the landowners.

04:33.082 --> 04:37.363
Most people whose farms were taken, they had about 90 days to leave the land.

04:38.624 --> 04:41.145
And then they basically...

04:43.606 --> 05:02.502
redistributed the land to other people either people from the city or government officials received land yeah hugely disruptive and really what it did Zimbabwe for people who don't know was had the reputation the name of being the breadbasket of Africa

05:04.363 --> 05:10.406
So we were producing enough food to feed the whole country, and we exported a ton of food to neighboring countries.

05:11.346 --> 05:20.651
And within three years, we were producing less than 20% of the food that we were producing three years before as a result of the nationalization.

05:21.871 --> 05:26.894
And Zimbabwe's economy at the time was largely driven on agriculture.

05:27.754 --> 05:51.997
and the economy imploded to the point that inflation was so bad that it was over 175 percent a day inflation was um it was it was a real disaster so basically the the country went bankrupt yeah yeah so thank you for the history yeah that is out yeah out to the people who yeah absolutely yeah um

05:55.535 --> 05:56.415
Sorry, just digesting.

05:56.476 --> 05:57.556
Yeah.

05:58.798 --> 06:04.443
So you said you were always interested in cattle and reproductive aspect of it.

06:04.463 --> 06:05.784
What kind of drew you to that?

06:06.771 --> 06:15.400
So growing up on a fairly large ranch and farm, we farmed and ranched.

06:16.301 --> 06:18.583
And so we had dairy cattle and we had beef cattle.

06:19.043 --> 06:27.692
And from a very young age, I'm not sure what it was that drew me to specifically reproduction in cattle.

06:27.972 --> 06:30.155
I was highly passionate about

06:31.236 --> 06:36.840
about livestock, I mean, from a very, very young age, as early as I can remember.

06:36.860 --> 06:39.822
I just loved working on the farm.

06:40.462 --> 06:46.747
But we had a neighbor that was one of the only people in the country who did

06:47.507 --> 06:48.508
embryo transfer.

06:48.769 --> 07:11.289
And embryo transfer is basically taking embryos from genetically superior animals and causing that cow to produce more than she normally does and then taking those embryos and transferring them to surrogates or recipients that are not as genetically good but they give birth to a genetically superior animal.

07:12.550 --> 07:40.685
and so I used to just I loved I mean when he was doing it on our farm it just sort of captured my attention and when I was in elementary school in fact nobody in my family can remember me ever not talking about specializing in reproduction right I was that that's what I my whole life that's what I was just passionate about and so throughout high school I

07:42.507 --> 07:45.809
I learned to artificially inseminate cows at a very young age.

07:45.910 --> 07:58.881
I was in high school, very early in high school, and very fortunate to have had the opportunity then to specialize in that through my career as a researcher.

07:58.941 --> 08:02.604
That's absolutely fascinating.

08:02.684 --> 08:07.068
It's not just that you're interested in cattle, but it's a very specific part of the cattle, too.

08:07.128 --> 08:07.449
Wow.

08:09.819 --> 08:14.953
What drew you to specifically the academic and research side of things rather than the industry side of things?

08:17.620 --> 08:46.430
really good question um to be perfectly honest my goal after i got a phd was to return and use the skills that i'd developed in growing agriculture back in zimbabwe and and that was to not only improve production efficiency of cattle operations beef cattle or dairy operations but i also had a passion in wildlife and so

08:47.090 --> 08:53.154
potentially utilizing the skills that I'd learned to then do some conservation work.

08:53.214 --> 08:58.098
So preserving genetics of endangered species and things like that.

09:00.546 --> 09:27.007
When I got word that I was not going to be able to go back and do anything on the farm or ranch, I sort of had a choice to either go into the U.S. industry on the commercial side, working with either a pharmaceutical company or a nutrition company or working in the industry somewhere or starting my own company,

09:28.548 --> 09:29.848
or to go into research.

09:30.708 --> 09:37.351
And while I was working on my PhD, I became really intrigued.

09:37.431 --> 09:42.833
It was a very interesting time of year for where I had an interest.

09:44.634 --> 09:54.419
And we were at that time developing a system to inseminate cows where we did not have to know when they were in heat.

09:54.679 --> 09:57.220
So we call it fixed-timed artificial insemination.

09:57.840 --> 10:05.144
So you take a group of cows, and typically you would have to make sure that the cow was receptive to become pregnant, right?

10:05.164 --> 10:05.924
She would be in heat.

10:05.944 --> 10:06.104
Right.

10:06.978 --> 10:16.304
And so if you had, say, 100 cows, if you wanted to get them all pregnant, you go out there and watch them and see when they come in heat, then you artificially inseminate them.

10:16.844 --> 10:26.529
We were in the process at that time of developing a system where you could inseminate them all at the same time on the same day, and it just makes things more efficient for a producer.

10:27.770 --> 10:48.170
So we'd started working on that while I was in my PhD, and I thought, you know, this is an opportunity to have a significant global impact in production systems for beef cattle where you can use reproduction to improve production efficiency of cattle operations.

10:48.310 --> 10:48.430
Yeah.

10:48.770 --> 11:11.351
And so the first 10 years of my research career, that really was my primary focus, is refining that system, working on products that were not approved in this country at the time, working to get them approved so that cattle producers had access to those products, and then being able to refine it.

11:15.956 --> 11:34.195
excuse me refine it in such a way that it was efficient for cattle producers to to use and you know you go through this process when you're working on your masters and phd that when you first start your masters you really don't understand what research is

11:35.456 --> 11:50.085
to when I finished my PhD, you know, I was really intrigued by the scientific method, the process, okay, and understanding all of the things that have been done and then coming up with ideas to make a difference in the future.

11:50.806 --> 11:56.729
And so by the time I finished my PhD, I could see myself in a role.

11:56.829 --> 11:58.370
Yeah, exactly.

11:59.211 --> 12:00.932
Is that system still in use today?

12:02.094 --> 12:02.635
Absolutely.

12:02.775 --> 12:06.661
So I'll give you some examples.

12:07.522 --> 12:16.455
You know, when we started, so between 1997 and 2003 is sort of when we really developed and refined these systems.

12:18.718 --> 12:27.087
More than half of the cows that are artificially inseminated in the U.S. today use the system we developed in 2003.

12:27.468 --> 12:30.070
And so that is happening today.

12:31.953 --> 12:38.039
And we also, in 2002, we started what's called the Beef Reproduction Task Force.

12:38.840 --> 12:42.344
And so it was scientists who work in the space around the country.

12:43.145 --> 12:45.908
A few of us got together to start the task force.

12:46.969 --> 12:58.102
And we came up with a set of recommendations for beef producers to use on what systems to use to get their cows pregnant to artificial insemination.

12:59.003 --> 13:06.325
about half of the protocols that are on that sheet were ones that we played, we developed or played a major role in developing.

13:06.425 --> 13:20.768
And so in the U.S., very, very fortunate that about 65 to 70 percent of all the heifers that are inseminated right now use a protocol that we had a hand in developing in some way.

13:21.548 --> 13:23.629
And then more than 50 percent of the cows

13:24.309 --> 13:24.809
That's so impressive.

13:26.169 --> 13:26.409
Yeah.

13:27.190 --> 13:34.991
But globally, I think that's even, is where we might have even had a greater role.

13:35.691 --> 13:44.793
And so you take a look, and 2002, 2003 was the first time I visited Brazil.

13:45.654 --> 13:51.355
And Brazil obviously is the next largest beef producer in the world behind the US.

13:52.575 --> 13:58.262
And we started talking about using these systems to get cows pregnant in Brazil.

13:59.043 --> 14:05.972
At the time, less than a million cows in Brazil were artificially inseminated through fixed-timed artificial insemination.

14:06.712 --> 14:09.496
Today, about 18 million.

14:11.599 --> 14:29.955
every year are inseminated using a a system or a variation of a system we developed in the late 90s and early 2000s so yeah so huge impact there in australia about 75 percent of beef cattle producers that are inseminated use the system we developed

14:31.007 --> 14:33.089
a global kind of impact.

14:33.149 --> 14:35.070
That is so impressive.

14:35.090 --> 14:44.377
What are some things that you've, new things added to it that you've seen in the years?

14:45.057 --> 14:55.545
Yeah, so like I said, in those first 10 years, sort of 97 to 2007, we spent a lot of time refining these systems.

14:55.945 --> 15:00.489
And I've continued to work in that space to even refine things a little bit further.

15:01.209 --> 15:14.213
But we've had the advent of the development of new technologies that then we incorporate into those types of systems.

15:14.253 --> 15:15.634
I'll give you one example.

15:16.634 --> 15:21.576
And very close to us here in Navasota is sexing technologies.

15:22.356 --> 15:27.903
It's a company that has a technology that can sex semen.

15:28.864 --> 15:41.459
And so you can inseminate a cow and the cow will give birth to over 90% accuracy that cow will give birth to a calf that is of a desired sex.

15:42.380 --> 15:58.719
So we took the systems we developed and developed fixed-timed AI systems where you could use sex semen because most of the systems we developed were with conventional semen, and those two products are very different in the way that they're utilized.

15:58.739 --> 15:58.899
Okay.

16:00.061 --> 16:14.856
possible yeah it is it's a it is yes yeah exactly and then obviously i have big interests in um in other reproductive technologies and so i've done a lot of work in in vitro fertilization

16:15.717 --> 16:32.602
a little bit in cloning and it's an area that excites me right now and i have graduate students and postdoc students right now that are working on we call it precision genetics so it's using crispr cas9 which is a gene editing technology

16:33.683 --> 16:40.131
to try and create animals that are more productive.

16:40.151 --> 16:45.999
I mean, we have calves on the ground right now that have been edited for the slick gene.

16:46.920 --> 16:50.245
And so just to explain how that works is that

16:52.166 --> 16:57.788
The slick gene is basically, it's a prolactin receptor.

16:58.428 --> 17:02.189
Prolactin is used for milk, is used for milk, right?

17:02.229 --> 17:04.709
It's a hormone that stimulates milk production.

17:04.869 --> 17:16.332
But at the same time, it's also tied to animals that have about 10% of the hair of the normal kind.

17:16.392 --> 17:18.413
And so we've edited those animals

17:19.333 --> 17:25.841
to have the slick genes so they have 10% of the hair of their full siblings.

17:25.901 --> 17:37.674
So full sibling, two brothers or two sisters can be standing side by side, and the one that's been edited for the slick gene has only 10% of the hair of the other one.

17:38.636 --> 17:39.917
And why is that important?

17:40.137 --> 17:43.000
Well, it's important because in Texas, it's really hot.

17:44.562 --> 17:53.452
And so if they have less hair, they don't spend as much energy trying to cool down during the warm times of the year.

17:55.259 --> 18:09.989
And so when we take the temperature of these animals during the heat of the day, those animals that have this slick gene have a body temperature that's about a degree to a degree and a half less

18:10.870 --> 18:32.720
in the middle of the day than those that have full hair that are full siblings so for warm climates you can ultimately breed these animals and they are more productive they survive better they get they're not as sick there's just a lot of these traits that will help them be more productive and so

18:34.401 --> 18:36.262
How creative.

18:36.942 --> 18:41.805
My first thoughts when I think of science, reproduction, I don't think creative, but that is so creative.

18:42.585 --> 18:43.086
That's right.

18:43.286 --> 18:44.166
Yeah, it is.

18:44.306 --> 18:49.609
And so that's a simple gene to edit.

18:50.289 --> 18:52.811
And obviously that's where we are right now.

18:54.472 --> 19:00.815
You can do an edit to make animals not have horns.

19:01.575 --> 19:02.156
And so...

19:03.076 --> 19:26.418
the reason that you would do that right now in the old days cows needed horns to fight off predators we really don't have to worry about that now but so what so what a lot of people do is they actually have to dehorn the animals manually which becomes it's just not a nice procedure right but if we breed them to not have horns then you don't even have to do that right

19:27.672 --> 19:28.974
Yeah.

19:29.875 --> 19:45.794
But my ultimate goal is to try and create edits that are multifaceted edits that sort of focuses on more than one gene and focus really on the disease side of things.

19:46.795 --> 20:01.491
and the reason for that is that i i want cattle to i want to be able to breed animals that are resistant to certain diseases or ailments that you don't have to treat so you think of what happens then

20:02.272 --> 20:05.495
They're more productive because they don't get sick.

20:07.596 --> 20:09.938
Fewer of them die, fewer of them get sick and so on.

20:10.619 --> 20:15.523
But in addition to that is we have a significant reduction in the use of antibiotics.

20:16.683 --> 20:22.008
And, you know, there's a lot of discussion about antibiotic resistance, right?

20:22.768 --> 20:27.332
And so how do we limit the use of antibiotics in animal agriculture?

20:27.372 --> 20:30.034
And this is one way that I see as a potential solution.

20:30.134 --> 20:31.175
That's so smart.

20:32.476 --> 20:36.961
As you said, two solutions, or two problems, one solution.

20:37.081 --> 20:39.063
One solution, yeah, exactly.

20:39.643 --> 20:43.607
Is there any things that you can share that your protégés are working on?

20:46.192 --> 20:58.417
Yeah, I mean, so right now we have graduate students that are working in anywhere from the really applied side of things.

20:58.477 --> 21:02.958
And when I say applied, it's things that are immediately applicable to a producer, right?

21:02.998 --> 21:07.300
So our results might have application to them this year or next year, right?

21:07.880 --> 21:16.924
And then we have some very fundamental things that results might only help us in 10 or 15 years from now.

21:17.064 --> 21:29.030
So I'll give you an example of something that we're working on in the short term, and that is utilizing different products

21:29.870 --> 21:54.403
to to give to cows around the start of the breeding season so we're not even worrying about whether they're artificially inseminated because still there are so many cows out that you can't handle very easily but if you could just have them once what can you do to treat them that they become pregnant sooner in the breeding season

21:54.983 --> 21:57.827
And they give birth to their calves sooner in the calving season.

21:58.629 --> 22:05.699
And those calves end up becoming a lot more productive than if they don't become pregnant sooner in the breeding season.

22:05.719 --> 22:09.084
So we're working on a couple of projects in that space.

22:10.666 --> 22:20.292
Then sort of in the intermediate space, we have a graduate student right now working on utilizing, doing in vitro fertilization.

22:23.654 --> 22:25.636
And we're using seminal plasma.

22:25.656 --> 22:37.543
So when a bull ejaculates semen, right, there's a lot of, not just the sperm, but there's a lot of fluids around the sperm that has a lot of components.

22:38.590 --> 22:47.975
Well, we have data from multiple bulls that are very high fertility, and we have bulls that are low fertility, right?

22:47.995 --> 22:49.135
And we have all of this data.

22:49.215 --> 23:05.004
And so what we've done is we're collecting seminal plasma from the two groups and then going to see if we can fertilize embryos and understand what it is about the seminal plasma in those two groups that causes differences in fertility.

23:05.724 --> 23:06.304
Does that make sense?

23:06.344 --> 23:07.385
Yeah, it does, absolutely.

23:08.005 --> 23:14.026
And then, obviously, the thing that I talked about is on the gene editing front.

23:14.686 --> 23:20.608
And, yeah, there are several things that we're working on on the gene editing front.

23:20.628 --> 23:22.948
That's very exciting.

23:23.408 --> 23:24.669
A layperson's question.

23:24.789 --> 23:25.109
Yes.

23:26.129 --> 23:31.190
So the insemination, when you inseminate all of them at once instead of having to wait,

23:31.788 --> 23:37.554
Does that create a situation where all of them are pregnant, thus give birth at the same time too?

23:38.155 --> 23:39.055
That's a great question.

23:39.196 --> 23:39.936
Yeah.

23:40.437 --> 23:43.660
So gestation of a cow is about 280 to 285 days.

23:43.680 --> 23:44.721
So about 10 days longer than a human.

23:48.444 --> 23:52.948
And so, yes, they're all pregnant, let's say, on the same day.

23:54.870 --> 24:02.957
And when they go to calve, they're usually spread out at the max about three weeks.

24:03.778 --> 24:05.720
So not unlike humans, right?

24:05.780 --> 24:11.485
Some humans give birth 10 days early and some humans give birth 10 days late.

24:11.545 --> 24:14.007
And so that would be very similar in cattle.

24:14.748 --> 24:39.243
when we do mass inseminate you get somewhere around 50 to 60 percent of them pregnant so not all of them will get pregnant you know and cattle are highly fertile because they've been selected for being fertile because if they don't produce a calf they consume resources so they're so they're eliminated right

24:39.983 --> 24:50.635
So that percentage is very high, 50 to 60 percent for a single offspring bearing species, like a cow that generally only gives birth to one.

24:52.517 --> 24:55.040
They usually only give birth to one animal at a time.

24:55.600 --> 24:57.622
So litters are very different, right?

24:57.642 --> 25:00.604
You can get a pig pregnant almost any time you inseminate her.

25:01.084 --> 25:05.467
You've just got to hope you get more piglets than one or two, right?

25:06.088 --> 25:07.589
But cows only give birth to one.

25:09.430 --> 25:20.578
In comparison, and I give this, I tell people this all the time, so 50% to 60% of those cows get pregnant, but humans are about half that.

25:22.200 --> 25:26.821
Fertility, humans are the least fertile species that give birth to one animal.

25:26.841 --> 25:27.221
Really?

25:27.361 --> 25:27.621
Yes.

25:28.521 --> 25:31.902
Because we don't select humans for fertility.

25:32.962 --> 25:36.963
And, you know, even in the wild, there's selection for fertility, right?

25:37.943 --> 25:38.583
Right.

25:39.143 --> 25:48.565
But humans, we try to figure out ways that every woman who wants to get pregnant, we try and get them pregnant in some way, right?

25:48.585 --> 25:48.805
Right.

25:50.086 --> 25:53.088
And so we're a very infertile species.

25:54.048 --> 25:56.129
That really puts it in perspective, too.

25:56.910 --> 26:04.815
And they think whatever, they think everything that they don't give birth all at the same time, because that would be a disaster.

26:04.855 --> 26:05.676
It would, yes.

26:05.956 --> 26:08.998
But it is a lot of people don't realize that

26:10.038 --> 26:26.130
that only about one in three times that they ovulate, and obviously through fertilization and things like that, only about one in three survive in humans.

26:26.930 --> 26:44.809
i did not know that either yeah few people know that yes because a lot of humans don't know that they're pregnant or there's a fertilized egg and the egg dies before they menstruate the next time but yeah it's yeah so yeah

26:45.770 --> 26:48.654
Thank you for going into the little nitty gritties of that.

26:50.155 --> 27:00.187
So when you were in Florida before Texas A&M, how did the opportunity arrive to you for this original position here?

27:00.728 --> 27:01.429
At Texas A&M, yeah.

27:03.471 --> 27:05.712
You know, it's a good question.

27:07.072 --> 27:11.934
I'll go back to when I did two sort of things here.

27:12.654 --> 27:22.978
When I was trying to decide where to go to university when I was growing up in Africa, I was very familiar with Texas A&M and Colorado State.

27:23.018 --> 27:25.259
Those are two universities that I was aware of.

27:25.899 --> 27:30.922
that really did a good job in the reproduction space in the world at the time.

27:32.143 --> 27:43.269
And so I actually applied to those two universities to come to study at that time and ended up going to Middle Tennessee State for a very different reason.

27:44.870 --> 27:48.753
So that was my first sort of thought of Texas A&M.

27:51.574 --> 27:54.156
I had no intention of being a university administrator.

27:54.676 --> 27:56.777
Never thought about it.

27:56.797 --> 28:01.598
It was never really something that I was trying to do.

28:03.519 --> 28:15.382
However, when I was at the University of Florida, I was an administrator with a faculty position, and there were several universities that were asking if I'd be interested in being a department ed.

28:17.667 --> 28:30.754
I had thought that if I was ever going to do that, the place that I would go and do it was at my alma mater, which was Kansas State, and was considering that because I knew the department head was retiring.

28:31.275 --> 28:37.618
At that time, I did get a call from Texas A&M that said, why don't you come and take a look?

28:38.178 --> 28:39.139
I considered it.

28:39.499 --> 28:40.440
I came and took a look.

28:42.501 --> 28:49.889
And I thought that there's probably no university that has the resources

28:51.086 --> 29:11.178
and the desire to be the best right and so after speaking with my wife and my family i said you know i'd be crazy not to take the opportunity because because i think i could make i mean give it the reputation of being the as being the best in the world so that's what that's how i came to the decision

29:11.838 --> 29:17.524
It's so funny to me how things from our past pop up again like that.

29:17.764 --> 29:19.506
You replied to it and it's here again.

29:19.606 --> 29:20.107
Exactly.

29:20.247 --> 29:20.867
Fate.

29:21.708 --> 29:24.291
Yeah, absolutely it was fate.

29:24.471 --> 29:24.931
That's right.

29:26.233 --> 29:32.259
So that decision, was that decision made when they brought you out and you sold the facilities and everything like that too?

29:32.279 --> 29:32.499
Sure.

29:32.779 --> 29:47.412
Yeah, so I think I took four trips out here during the process of when they interviewed me by phone to when I finally took the position.

29:50.194 --> 29:52.035
You know, we had kids that were in high school.

29:52.055 --> 29:57.137
I had to make sure that they were comfortable moving here at the time.

29:57.437 --> 30:13.385
And then at the same time, I wanted to learn more about what are some of the things that seem to be, should I say, slowing them down or not giving them the opportunity to

30:14.345 --> 30:21.008
be recognized outside of Texas as one of the better animal science departments in the country or the world.

30:21.969 --> 30:29.413
And, you know, most of that stuff was probably just decisions that I thought I could make.

30:29.433 --> 30:31.514
And, yeah, and we did.

30:32.835 --> 30:33.395
Fantastic.

30:33.415 --> 30:33.635
Yeah.

30:35.896 --> 30:43.140
So when you arrived here, what were your first impressions of the department as well as just the broader Agile culture?

30:46.196 --> 30:47.758
What am I trying to say?

30:47.798 --> 30:48.498
The College of Agriculture.

30:48.839 --> 30:49.119
Yes.

30:50.040 --> 30:58.508
So the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences is the college in which the Department of Animal Science sits.

30:59.269 --> 31:03.073
And so it's one of 15 departments.

31:05.455 --> 31:18.287
The things that I, so first arriving here, the things that I thought were things that they could improve on, right, is fantastic culture, right?

31:18.488 --> 31:22.111
Everybody who graduates from A&M is proud to be from A&M.

31:23.873 --> 31:27.756
And it has this significant impact.

31:28.597 --> 31:50.828
and in their future professions and you know it's a network a lot of people say it's a cult right but it's a very good cult it's a nice cult there are some things that are just very quirky about texas a m that make it unique which which i really enjoy however the one thing that i felt like is that um

31:52.858 --> 32:16.997
that previous decisions in terms of who was hired it tended to be they all tended to have ties to texas a and m and i felt like the way that texas a and m is going to grow especially that department is to consciously find the best person out there and it didn't matter where they're from they could be from some other country

32:18.178 --> 32:21.201
It could be from a different state or different university.

32:22.001 --> 32:33.010
But we needed to broaden the horizon and bring in the best people rather than just saying we have the best people because we taught them here.

32:33.711 --> 32:38.115
Change the way we think about people, change the way that we teach in the classes.

32:39.916 --> 32:41.437
You know, we revamped the...

32:42.718 --> 32:45.621
We revised the curriculum in the department too.

32:47.723 --> 33:00.113
So shortly after I arrived and some of the legwork had already been done before I got here, but we pushed it forward where we wanted more hands-on experiences, things like that, having more labs.

33:00.953 --> 33:06.599
And it's complicated because it's very difficult for a university to have those resources.

33:07.160 --> 33:17.811
But we felt it was going to be important for us to have more hands-on opportunities, ensure we had internship opportunities,

33:19.853 --> 33:21.135
animal experiences.

33:21.155 --> 33:24.559
Actually get out of the classroom.

33:25.000 --> 33:28.584
Yeah, get out the classroom and learn a little bit more beyond the classroom.

33:29.325 --> 33:30.887
Those are all things we implemented.

33:30.907 --> 33:32.549
How is that resolved?

33:33.855 --> 33:44.751
Oh, I think, you know, obviously it was received well because the feedback we got in that process said that that's a big need.

33:45.311 --> 33:46.914
The students like the opportunity.

33:48.396 --> 33:49.237
But what they're really...

33:50.338 --> 33:57.747
took to is some of the new faculty members that we brought in that may not have been Aggies brought a different perspective, right?

33:57.827 --> 34:02.272
And so we changed the perspective, right?

34:02.332 --> 34:03.554
Yeah, exactly.

34:04.054 --> 34:06.577
Do you remember some of those first people that you brought in?

34:07.598 --> 34:35.968
yes i do absolutely and you know it is not not easy for many of them to integrate right but you know they knew that they had my support and i think that that probably helped and and many of those faculty are thriving they're doing extremely well that's fantastic but they're still here yeah that's right that shows that it worked right there yeah because people don't stay in places that they're not happy exactly yeah um

34:37.328 --> 34:43.113
So what were some key questions that you set out to answer throughout your work?

34:43.453 --> 34:48.918
I mean, we've gone over some of them, but even now for questions for the future.

34:50.559 --> 34:53.401
Yeah, so I can share this in a couple of...

34:54.282 --> 35:05.872
So from my research background, some key questions, which we've talked about, is how do you use reproduction to improve production efficiency?

35:06.632 --> 35:20.098
When we hear people talking about sustainability of agricultural systems today, everybody talks about methane and all of these things, right?

35:20.558 --> 35:23.419
And how do you reduce methane in animals?

35:25.060 --> 35:30.522
And there's talks on climate change and the impacts on water and all of those things.

35:31.322 --> 35:39.414
But the number one factor that affects sustainability of livestock operations is getting them pregnant.

35:40.475 --> 35:47.406
And if they're not pregnant, it doesn't matter how much less they consume to produce more or how much less methane or whatever.

35:49.607 --> 35:50.908
they're inefficient, right?

35:51.128 --> 35:53.289
And they're not sustainable.

35:54.209 --> 36:01.013
So using reproduction to increase sustainability of livestock production systems, and we still work on that today.

36:01.693 --> 36:12.979
That's an interesting idea from somebody from the outside that I didn't even think about, that the more efficient the reproduction is, the more sustainable it is.

36:13.219 --> 36:13.719
Absolutely.

36:14.960 --> 36:16.981
The common person doesn't put those two things together often.

36:17.594 --> 36:18.054
Exactly.

36:18.374 --> 36:18.575
Yeah.

36:19.355 --> 36:28.519
You know, and I feel bad that, you know, for some reason, people have taken cows or ruminants.

36:28.599 --> 36:31.600
It's not just cows, but certainly cows are the big thing.

36:32.220 --> 36:43.965
But sheep and goats and said, you know, they're producing greenhouse gases and really it's such a small part of the total greenhouse gas emissions.

36:44.685 --> 36:51.111
that even if we got rid of every cow, we wouldn't change greenhouse gas emissions by more than half a percent, right?

36:51.792 --> 36:55.636
And so if you get rid of every cow, it's not going to change much.

36:56.537 --> 37:07.907
Unfortunately, the general population doesn't understand that because 98% of the population does not work directly in agriculture in this country.

37:07.927 --> 37:08.628
Correct.

37:08.968 --> 37:10.910
And we have a social media.

37:10.930 --> 37:19.816
I mean, everybody makes up their mind on very small snippets of things that they get to believe.

37:19.896 --> 37:21.317
So it's really unfortunate.

37:21.357 --> 37:22.058
There's no context behind it.

37:22.078 --> 37:22.178
Yeah.

37:22.198 --> 37:22.898
That makes sense.

37:24.685 --> 37:24.885
Yeah.

37:25.185 --> 37:30.687
So, you know, questions that I ask now in my role as director of AgriLife Research.

37:30.727 --> 37:38.289
So AgriLife Research is it's a state agency within the Texas A&M University system.

37:38.969 --> 37:42.590
You know, we're on the A&M campus, but we don't just support A&M.

37:42.630 --> 37:51.153
We support all 12 universities and we have locations all over the state.

37:53.534 --> 38:12.260
But yeah, so questions that I really focus on now is how do we continue to figure out ways to produce more food for a growing population of Texas on less and less arable land?

38:13.660 --> 38:21.603
And Texas is particularly different from any other state because 95% of the land in Texas is privately owned.

38:24.209 --> 38:25.971
I do not know that.

38:25.991 --> 38:26.551
Yeah.

38:26.711 --> 38:31.094
So most other states have more public land than Texas does as a percentage.

38:32.595 --> 38:37.338
And we have about 1,500 people moving into Texas every day.

38:38.679 --> 38:43.122
So that's 1,500 extra mouths we have to feed.

38:44.823 --> 38:47.204
The population of Texas is...

38:48.365 --> 38:50.006
over 33 million right now.

38:52.507 --> 39:02.312
And 85% of the population is probably urban, which leaves about 15% more rural and living in more rural regions of the state.

39:03.513 --> 39:10.917
But the problem we have is that we had a lot more arable land previously or land for livestock.

39:13.758 --> 39:15.820
Texas has done very well economically.

39:15.940 --> 39:21.224
People who live in Texas have more resources, so they want a little bit more land.

39:21.485 --> 39:26.248
And so they are now purchasing arable land.

39:26.529 --> 39:35.436
And you see urban regions that were once land that was producing food on, right?

39:35.456 --> 39:36.137
Oh, no.

39:36.917 --> 39:48.723
And so you see all of this segmentation of land going on in Texas that is just gradually eating away at our arable land that we produce the food to feed the population on.

39:48.903 --> 39:49.464
That's concerning.

39:49.964 --> 39:50.324
It is.

39:50.644 --> 39:58.408
And so from my standpoint as director of agri-life research is how do we become even more efficient

39:59.689 --> 40:20.427
on less and less land and to feed a growing population and then the other thing that we have a major problem with is water so you know there's competition now and more and more competition for water for people that is taking it away from

40:22.058 --> 40:24.761
The agricultural use water, right?

40:25.482 --> 40:39.078
And you take a look at what is happening in the Rio Grande Valley, where we've lost an industry like the sugarcane industry is gone because there was not enough water for sugarcane.

40:39.098 --> 40:39.498
Wow.

40:39.918 --> 40:49.783
You go further up the Rio Grande towards West Texas, El Paso, and that region, and a lot of those crops can't be grown.

40:49.823 --> 40:50.883
That used to be grown there.

40:50.963 --> 40:51.523
Vegetables.

40:51.603 --> 40:53.444
It used to be a huge vegetable area.

40:54.525 --> 41:01.208
We can't grow vegetables because the water has become so brackish that they can't put it on the soil, so they can't grow vegetables.

41:02.048 --> 41:04.209
So it's not just cattle?

41:04.370 --> 41:06.191
It's all production, yeah.

41:06.611 --> 41:12.955
And then you go up into the High Plains, so where most of the cattle production is.

41:14.996 --> 41:16.737
So this is where the feedlots are.

41:17.758 --> 41:22.721
Texas is now the third largest dairy state in the country.

41:23.581 --> 41:29.065
And they get their water largely from the Ogallala Aquifer.

41:29.905 --> 41:33.127
And it's not recharging at the rates it should.

41:33.247 --> 41:38.470
So how is that going to impact agriculture 20, 30, 40 years from now?

41:38.810 --> 41:42.192
And so we're trying to find solutions for that.

41:42.212 --> 41:54.479
I have a question, and if you don't want to touch this topic, the first thing that popped in my head when you were talking about limited resources, especially water, is data centers and the conversation around that and water.

41:54.780 --> 41:55.000
Yes.

41:55.740 --> 41:59.227
Is there a meeting or a plan kind of thing?

41:59.683 --> 42:08.009
Yeah, so you may not be aware, but so within AgriLife Research, we have multiple entities that we run.

42:09.269 --> 42:14.573
One entity is called the Texas Water Resource Institute, and it's a statewide effort.

42:14.753 --> 42:19.696
I have a director that reports up to me, and we have these discussions all the time.

42:20.377 --> 42:26.241
So data centers are, you know, there's a lot of discussions about data centers and the use of water.

42:27.241 --> 42:49.768
um and then you have the oil and gas industry that is utilizing a lot of or generating a lot of it's called produced water it's water that is part of the fracking industry right so that's additional water that we are actually getting but it's not very clean you can't drink it right and so

42:50.928 --> 43:04.236
So the discussions we're having right now are can you use some of this water that people can't drink for data centers, for agricultural land, for things like that?

43:04.256 --> 43:04.356
Yeah.

43:06.518 --> 43:11.141
so that you save the potable or the drinking water for the human population.

43:11.482 --> 43:13.583
So that's work that's going on right now.

43:13.843 --> 43:15.685
See, that makes me feel better.

43:16.305 --> 43:21.269
That's something I hope gets put out into the public and they care about that.

43:21.930 --> 43:22.750
Yeah, that's right.

43:23.671 --> 43:33.379
Major concerns, right, with these data centers and storage facilities, right, energy storage facilities.

43:35.059 --> 43:49.323
is water is how they're cooled and how do we utilize water in such a way that it doesn't take away from the current needs.

43:50.683 --> 43:55.024
Can you talk about maybe a project or a finding or something that's

43:56.180 --> 44:03.523
You feel that's a particularly important impact on the production practices here, or at large?

44:04.023 --> 44:05.283
Yeah.

44:07.324 --> 44:14.926
Well, maybe I can touch on one that I think is really important that we're trying to resolve, right?

44:16.147 --> 44:18.227
A lot of people don't realize that Texas has

44:21.541 --> 44:27.351
the largest land ports of entry for agricultural products into the US.

44:28.739 --> 44:45.063
So you can go to Brownwood, Brownsville, sorry, Laredo or El Paso and agricultural products are coming into the US through Mexico.

44:45.543 --> 44:47.543
And then we've got the seaports on the Gulf.

44:47.823 --> 44:51.624
So Corpus Christi, Houston, Galveston.

44:52.104 --> 44:55.825
So you've got all of these agricultural products coming into the US

44:56.965 --> 45:09.656
And at any day, there could be a pathogen, there could be a disease that comes into the U.S. that we don't have a solution for.

45:10.057 --> 45:19.525
And so we spend a lot of time trying to figure out how do we prevent that stuff from being imported.

45:19.605 --> 45:23.349
Because once it gets into Texas, then it can spread through the rest of the U.S.

45:23.969 --> 45:31.894
There's no other state that brings as many and as much agricultural products into the U.S. than we do in Texas.

45:32.774 --> 45:43.261
And so whether it's livestock or crops or vegetables or fruits, whatever it is, there could be a fly on a fruit that comes in.

45:43.901 --> 45:52.887
People don't realize this, but if it gets in and then it spreads, it could destroy some other agricultural industry that we're unaware of.

45:53.407 --> 45:54.828
And so I'll give you some examples.

45:55.508 --> 45:59.431
Right now, you may have heard of the New World Screwworm.

46:00.431 --> 46:03.854
So we're at the forefront of fighting the screwworm.

46:04.374 --> 46:08.897
Our goal is to figure out ways to keep the screwworm from entering the U.S.

46:09.897 --> 46:16.439
Right now, the closest we have a detected screw worm is about 60 miles from the border.

46:17.059 --> 46:19.440
But today that could change, right?

46:20.060 --> 46:26.422
But it's moved all the way from Panama, all the way up to 60 miles from the U.S. border.

46:27.042 --> 46:41.934
So if the screw room gets here and you talked about a production practice, right, it will change the way that livestock and wildlife producers produce their product in South Texas.

46:42.635 --> 46:44.576
It will significantly impact them.

46:45.817 --> 47:04.137
um you know because basically a screw worm is a female only mates once but she'll plant the egg in an open wound of healthy flesh and then that screw basically the lava is just like a screw and it just drills itself into this live flesh

47:05.178 --> 47:23.930
so it's a lot of work and it usually is a wound so it could be where an animal has a bite or there's some infection or some type of laceration or something like that and so on the beef production side when an animal is born where the umbilical cord is

47:24.931 --> 47:26.692
is a perfect place for a screw worm.

47:27.012 --> 47:31.594
Or if you have to dehorn an animal or take the horns off, you create open wounds there.

47:32.574 --> 47:45.000
On the wildlife side, which might be worse than on the livestock side, is when the males are rutting or when they're fighting.

47:45.980 --> 47:50.601
that's the opportunity for them to significantly have screw up.

47:51.122 --> 47:58.564
So it's a it's a huge concern, but it's going to change agricultural production in Texas if it crosses the border.

47:59.344 --> 47:59.524
Yeah.

48:00.464 --> 48:06.066
And when it was here 50, 60, 70 years ago, it did not.

48:08.266 --> 48:10.647
We did not have the wildlife industry we do now.

48:10.787 --> 48:13.148
Most of the landowners in South Texas

48:14.328 --> 48:21.595
were making their money primarily from agriculture, from raising livestock and selling livestock.

48:24.811 --> 48:28.934
it's really difficult just to have a single source of income like livestock.

48:28.974 --> 48:36.538
And so they either have they make money from livestock, wildlife and hunting or oil and gas.

48:37.098 --> 48:40.801
So those three forms in some way, I shouldn't say oil and gas.

48:40.841 --> 48:43.442
It could be renewable energy also.

48:43.803 --> 48:45.724
So wind, solar, things like that.

48:46.484 --> 48:48.145
So there's sort of three forms of income.

48:48.545 --> 48:54.069
If you think that the screwworm gets in here and has an impact on cattle and an impact on wildlife,

48:54.971 --> 48:58.697
Right, that's going to be a significant drain on the landowners in Texas.

48:58.757 --> 49:00.160
That could crash.

49:00.180 --> 49:00.340
Yep.

49:00.981 --> 49:03.585
Absolutely.

49:03.605 --> 49:07.472
Someone from outside of Texas, whenever the screwworm first hit,

49:11.440 --> 49:24.884
Yeah, so and right now as of today, but like I said, I get an update every week As of today, we don't have screw worm in the US.

49:25.444 --> 49:35.867
Okay, and Some some people believe we do but we do not have screw worm in the US Every now and then a human might come back with a screw worm or something

49:37.247 --> 49:45.110
but right now there's no endemic population of screwworms, knock on wood.

49:46.590 --> 49:55.973
The way that it was pushed back in the 1950s, 60s and 70s is the sterile fly technology.

49:56.273 --> 50:00.535
So because a screwworm female only mates once,

50:02.695 --> 50:28.980
we can use irradiation and so we can irradiate lava before they become flies to be sterile okay yeah and so essentially what was done is basically they irradiated these uh these lava they became flies they were not sterile and then those were deployed

50:29.700 --> 50:47.782
where the screw worms were and they deployed enough of them that they over overwhelmed just based on numbers right they overwhelm the population of wild screw worm flies and so it was mostly the sterile males mating

50:48.748 --> 51:16.854
the females and then they basically it wiped them out and pushed them all the way down to Mexico sorry down to Panama through Mexico down to Panama but so there were a lot of these plants so there was a plant in south Texas a plant in Mexico a plant in Nicaragua a plant in Panama that was producing sterile flies every week and that's how they got rid of them

51:17.578 --> 51:23.501
Well, what happened is they stopped, they closed down all of those facilities except for the one in Panama.

51:24.221 --> 51:30.404
And as the screw room was moving north, the Panama facility just couldn't produce enough sterile flies.

51:30.964 --> 51:32.125
That makes sense.

51:32.585 --> 51:37.488
So they're building a new facility in Texas right now to be able to.

51:37.868 --> 51:38.548
Just in case.

51:38.588 --> 51:41.910
Just, yes, to be able to help push those flies back.

51:41.970 --> 51:44.431
Thank you for explaining that to me.

51:44.491 --> 51:45.471
Yeah.

51:46.392 --> 51:46.632
Yeah.

51:47.808 --> 51:55.814
Is there ways your research has changed or evolved over the course of your career as new technologies and new challenges have arrived?

51:56.695 --> 51:56.935
Yeah.

51:56.955 --> 52:03.160
So from my standpoint, I'll talk about it from my research and then as an administrator.

52:03.440 --> 52:03.620
Yeah.

52:04.381 --> 52:13.387
So, yeah, there is no question that my focus has changed over time.

52:14.648 --> 52:31.266
As we have, I shouldn't say perfected, but as we've refined processes, and I talked about fixed-timed artificial insemination, and I continue to do work in this space, but the improvements are only incremental, right?

52:32.107 --> 52:40.377
And so I've tried to find other ways to enhance reproductive efficiency, right?

52:40.417 --> 52:50.969
And so one of the areas that's important to me is the only way you can generally increase pregnancy or have more cows give birth

52:51.810 --> 52:56.276
is to reduce the number of animals that don't lose a pregnancy, right?

52:56.736 --> 53:00.921
So focusing on trying to understand the fundamental reason around that.

53:01.602 --> 53:06.208
And then I've always had a passion in sort of the...

53:09.010 --> 53:10.251
the genetic side of things.

53:10.271 --> 53:11.613
How can we improve the genetics?

53:11.733 --> 53:26.507
And that's why gene editing and, you know, with the advent and development of CRISPR-Cas9 and the advent of the first clone sheep, Dolly, at the Roslyn Institute in

53:27.428 --> 53:37.374
the UK, that technology has given us a chance to combine those two technologies to generate gene-edited cattle.

53:37.694 --> 53:39.335
And so that's pretty cool.

53:40.016 --> 53:41.256
That's so neat.

53:41.657 --> 53:46.460
It makes me excited to see what kind of technology comes out in the future and how that impacts that.

53:47.360 --> 53:57.414
But as an administrator, I think you go through these phases of where new technologies incrementally change the way you do business.

53:58.176 --> 54:00.919
Right now, we are in a phase of

54:03.300 --> 54:27.427
if we're going to increase our efficiency of food production we're in a phase right now where we need to significantly and significantly advance our work in the use of artificial intelligence machine learning sensor technology and things like that that develop

54:28.267 --> 54:41.748
aids whether they're decision tools or technologies that vastly change the way that we do business and so ai is going to change agriculture for the future

54:42.306 --> 54:43.027
Have you ever seen it?

54:43.567 --> 54:43.887
Oh, yes.

54:44.768 --> 54:47.110
Yeah, I've got several great examples, right?

54:47.190 --> 55:01.061
And so we have our big digital ag group at our Corpus Christi Center has developed a model using drones and satellites.

55:02.816 --> 55:09.820
and big data and taking weather, past and future forecast weather data.

55:10.620 --> 55:21.286
And they predicted, this would have been two years ago, that cotton farmers in the region should harvest their cotton three weeks earlier than they ever have done before.

55:22.587 --> 55:30.912
And those that actually did it got a 10% increase in the total cotton value of their crops than they'd ever got before.

55:32.612 --> 55:42.322
One producer didn't believe it, and he harvested his cotton sometime after July 4th is when they do it down there.

55:43.243 --> 55:49.129
And he told me that he said because he didn't listen, it was about a $375,000 loss by not doing it.

55:53.333 --> 56:03.636
So so there's a tangible example of how artificial intelligence, machine learning and all of the data science can significantly change.

56:04.656 --> 56:06.076
I could make or break somebody.

56:06.336 --> 56:06.877
Absolutely.

56:07.537 --> 56:12.318
On the animal side, you know, we all walk around with phones.

56:12.418 --> 56:15.139
We walk around with watches that keep track of.

56:16.222 --> 56:28.988
Heart rate and all of this stuff, you've seen these aura rings that people wear, right, that are always monitoring something about them, whether it's blood pressure, heart rates, things like that.

56:30.788 --> 56:52.887
um we're starting to use this technology right and it goes back to trying to identify animals that might be sick before they even act sick so traditionally how we would do that is somebody's looking at the animals every day says oh that animal's got droopy ears it looks like it's not healthy we need to go and get its temperature and treat it

56:53.848 --> 57:15.968
well we're using this technology now to try and identify animals that are not doing well before they show clinical signs you can treat them sooner they recover quicker you use less antibiotics you use less treatments and that way you use have less

57:17.509 --> 57:44.236
