01:25:50.677 --> 01:26:08.262
also got into a situation where red meat is bad for you right fat is bad for you so um so the cattle industry is changing and adjusting to that and now we're in a situation where thank goodness

01:26:09.221 --> 01:26:17.808
people have started actually seeing the science and it's not necessarily the red meat, it's the carbohydrates, it's bread and sugar and things, right?

01:26:19.189 --> 01:26:24.433
Where protein, animal protein actually has a lot of really good benefits, right?

01:26:25.674 --> 01:26:33.800
And so now we're in a market where marbling in cattle is extremely important.

01:26:33.820 --> 01:26:35.541
Marbling is the intramuscular fat.

01:26:36.713 --> 01:27:00.828
that actually helps us have more tender products yep exactly wagyu and then most angus genetics products um and so when people cook that meat it's more difficult to screw it up and so they have a better eating experience right and and now we also have

01:27:03.009 --> 01:27:29.087
people that make more money and willing to pay more for their for better quality beef right so the producers that are not have not started producing animals that have better genetics the cattle markets have now shifted that were in the 80s and 90s it didn't matter what size animal went through through the sale barn and to slaughter

01:27:30.007 --> 01:27:56.453
everyone every animal is worth the same price based on weight that's not the case anymore an animal that was really good marbling genetics is worth about a thousand dollars more than an animal that isn't right yeah so can you imagine being a producer that was producing poor quality beef right that in the 80s and 90s might have been doing a good job and so they didn't adjust so so it's really difficult to pinpoint what the ideal operation is

01:27:57.693 --> 01:28:21.661
and every country has a different system but at the same time I think the ideal operation is the operation that is willing to adjust to what the consumer wants and the market signals quicker I didn't think that was ever going to have an answer because it's too complicated it is

01:28:27.992 --> 01:28:28.733
Are there any...

01:28:29.093 --> 01:28:41.962
I know we talked about technology and changes in the way we approach things, but is there something that really excites you now that you see coming into the future in new technologies or process?

01:28:43.763 --> 01:28:50.667
Yeah, I think we touched on this a little bit, but I do think what really excites me right now is...

01:28:54.154 --> 01:28:57.255
I think we're starting to see that people value where their food comes from.

01:28:58.715 --> 01:29:10.519
And then coupled with that is the exponential increase in our ability to use data to make decisions and decision aid tools and tools, right?

01:29:10.879 --> 01:29:20.501
So AI, machine learning, sensor technology, and then a consumer starting to value where their food comes from.

01:29:20.842 --> 01:29:22.142
Yeah, I think those two.

01:29:25.307 --> 01:29:30.311
And these last couple questions are like personal reflection kind of questions.

01:29:32.974 --> 01:29:41.419
Has there been any turning points in your career, whether it's good or bad, that significantly changed the trajectory of your life or career in general?

01:29:42.800 --> 01:29:47.623
Yeah, so, yeah, multiple, right?

01:29:47.783 --> 01:29:53.867
I think, and this might be something, you know, back to the mentoring, right?

01:29:53.887 --> 01:29:55.187
One of the things I tell my...

01:29:56.308 --> 01:30:16.641
students all the time when they're trying to make decisions is is it the right decision it's not really the right decision I mean when you think it's Yogi Berra said when you come to a fork in the road take it right so you're going to come to multiple times in your life where you have decisions to make

01:30:17.790 --> 01:30:19.811
Don't sit there and pine over the decision.

01:30:19.851 --> 01:30:21.893
Make the decision and don't look back, right?

01:30:22.713 --> 01:30:39.905
So in my life, I mean, I think a decision at a very young age, not knowing anybody in the U.S. and coming to study as a teenager to leave a family in Africa, right?

01:30:39.986 --> 01:30:40.246
Right.

01:30:41.651 --> 01:30:45.436
And not knowing what the future held, but that my plan was always to go back.

01:30:46.177 --> 01:30:51.946
So that was one, I think, not necessarily having...

01:30:54.205 --> 01:30:59.868
The opportunity to go back to Africa was a big turning point in my career.

01:31:00.468 --> 01:31:02.349
And don't regret it at all.

01:31:03.230 --> 01:31:04.530
It was sad when it happened.

01:31:04.770 --> 01:31:12.214
But I think I've been able to have a larger impact, even on my home country, doing what I'm doing than had I gone back.

01:31:12.234 --> 01:31:13.094
That's going to feel good.

01:31:14.515 --> 01:31:34.772
yeah yeah that does um and then probably the the last really big decision was i never really thought of being a university administrator right um never crossed my mind it was never something that i aspired to do

01:31:36.215 --> 01:32:01.894
but taking the leap when somebody asked me if I would consider it I think has been has been life-changing and you know I look at I look back at it now and every administrative position I've had I've been able to have a positive impact on more and more people and so so those are the probably the big big ones

01:32:08.482 --> 01:32:20.126
You've already given some advice, but is there any other kind of advice that you would like to give to people who maybe are interested in this field or just interested in this field?

01:32:24.528 --> 01:32:28.809
Yeah, I think there is some advice, right?

01:32:28.949 --> 01:32:36.092
I think the folks that will make the biggest impact in the future

01:32:37.891 --> 01:33:05.388
in in my my field and i'll just talk about animal science in general um and and maybe even over the entire agri-life research right the thing that i would say are those that are going to be the most successful are going to be the people who are willing to adapt faster um and i'll give you an example

01:33:06.923 --> 01:33:26.442
in the past if we were going to look for a faculty member or look for somebody to lead a certain area we would look we would say well we need somebody who's a geneticist right and then we go out and look for somebody who fits a position description

01:33:28.278 --> 01:33:54.639
but now when we do that yeah we may broadly want a geneticist but really what we want is we want somebody who over the next 30 to 40 years is going to be able to adapt to needs over that period of time because if we hire if we hire a geneticist today 30 years from now genetics may not be the focus that we need right we need somebody who's willing to adapt as time changes

01:33:55.740 --> 01:34:01.226
So I think adaptability is extremely important for anybody.

01:34:03.628 --> 01:34:11.095
For young people, I would say most of them want to come out and make a lot of money right off the bat.

01:34:11.776 --> 01:34:13.597
Almost everybody wants to do that.

01:34:14.198 --> 01:34:17.741
And I would let everybody know to be patient, right?

01:34:19.018 --> 01:34:44.543
um make sure that you spend your first five or ten years out of school honing your skills you may not it may not be the most the greatest income generator you may not become a millionaire in your first five or ten years but build on that and then gradually grow through the system and and and take on opportunities yeah don't rush it yeah

01:34:46.152 --> 01:34:54.516
I think we have too many people that want to leave and go and start a job right away because they see the dollar signs, but they don't see the investments in themselves.

01:34:55.937 --> 01:34:57.417
That's fantastic.

01:34:57.798 --> 01:34:59.458
I have two legacy questions.

01:35:00.119 --> 01:35:02.900
They've been asked for the last 50 years in these oral histories.

01:35:03.380 --> 01:35:11.484
The first is, if you were given a million dollars and you had to use it somehow on this university, does it matter where it went?

01:35:11.524 --> 01:35:12.204
Just your pick.

01:35:12.384 --> 01:35:13.445
What would you put that to you?

01:35:14.472 --> 01:35:34.904
If I had a million dollars, I would probably put it into an endowment so that it would live forever and use the interest from that to focus on graduate education.

01:35:36.369 --> 01:35:38.171
First person who says that to me, by the way.

01:35:38.411 --> 01:35:38.732
What's that?

01:35:39.052 --> 01:35:40.894
You're the first person who's told me that.

01:35:41.015 --> 01:35:41.755
Oh, really?

01:35:41.775 --> 01:35:42.156
Yeah, that's.

01:35:42.276 --> 01:35:42.556
Yes.

01:35:43.458 --> 01:35:44.259
So you need cancer.

01:35:44.419 --> 01:35:45.881
Yeah.

01:35:45.901 --> 01:35:50.526
And my very last question is, how do you want to be remembered?

01:35:51.187 --> 01:35:51.508
Mm-hmm.

01:35:52.430 --> 01:36:14.878
in your career or personal life that's a good question um i don't know i mean i haven't really thought about how do i want to be remembered i mean my hope is that when i'm when i hang up my shoes and i'm six feet under that people will remember me for having an impact on other people's lives

01:36:15.558 --> 01:36:31.295
yeah so yeah i mean i think the accolades in terms of science and things like that i think those are great but i think that's not really what drives me it's it's how do we impact other people's lives you know and and i really want them to

01:36:32.883 --> 01:36:37.568
remember that I was hugely passionate about agriculture.

01:36:37.808 --> 01:36:47.478
This is the first time I've physically met you in the flesh, and I can even feel that, so I'm sure that's going to be it.

01:36:47.498 --> 01:36:49.159
It's going to be your legacy.

01:36:50.941 --> 01:36:53.524
Is there any questions or comments that you wanted to...

01:36:55.624 --> 01:36:57.126
No, I think we covered a ton, didn't we?

01:36:57.146 --> 01:36:57.887
Yeah, I think so.

01:36:57.907 --> 01:36:58.227
Yes.

01:36:58.688 --> 01:37:00.611
Thank you so much for doing this that way.

01:37:00.631 --> 01:37:01.231
Oh, any time.

01:37:01.272 --> 01:37:01.472
Yeah.

